  prompt. sessionsation refused both sends because it couldn't
  recognise the screen layout, before typing anything. As you
  saw, the input was already usable, so the screen check was
  wrong. Auto Start still recorded the prompt as delivered,
  because it only looked at whether the send was accepted, not
  what happened to it.

  The fix, sessionsation 1.44.1:
  - [0m[1mDelivered means delivered:[0m the prompt only counts once the
    message is actually written or accepted.
  - [0m[1mRetries:[0m refusals where nothing was typed get retried for up
    to about 3 minutes.
  - [0m[1mClaude delivery:[0m Claude sessions get the prompt through
    Claude's own messaging channel first.
  - [0m[1mScreen check: [0mwhatever made the check misread your ready
    screen gets fixed.

  [0m[1m[38;2;177;185;249mprotected[0m[1m still showing Unmountable:[0m at boot Unraid did find
  both drives and the filesystem, then refused to mount it with
  "no pool uuid". When I built the pool by hand, the filesystem
  ID never got written into Unraid's pool settings. 
  - [0m[1mSettings:[0m I've added that ID to [0m[38;2;177;185;249mpools/protected.cfg[0m, with a
    backup. I also added a small hook, installed at boot, that
    puts it back if Unraid ever blanks it before mounting. 
  - [0m[1mMounted by hand for now: [0m[38;2;177;185;249m/mnt/protected [0mis mounted again so
    the backups are reachable. Both drives are present and there
    are no errors.
  - [0m[1mWhat I can't do yet:[0m the webui label only updates when the
    array next starts. Stopping it now would close every session
    and pane, this one included. With the new Auto Start rules,
    a stop and start without a reboot doesn't restart them. So,
    your call: say the word and I'll stop and start the array
    now (you'd then restart the sessions yourself), or I'll
    check it at your next reboot.

  The drives also changed names after the move (the WD is now
  [0m[38;2;177;185;249mnvme2n1[0m). The WD's 256 MB erase-chunk setting was still
  applied, because it matches by serial number, not by name.

